= Cloves (Syzygium aromaticum)

_Scope: This entry treats_ Syzygium aromaticum _(L.) Merr. & L.M.Perry — the clove tree of the Maluku (Moluccas) — and specifically its_ dried unopened flower buds, _the culinary-and-medicinal drug. The tradition also uses two minor derived drugs, flagged where relevant:_ mother cloves _(anthophylli — the ripe fruit; Chinese 母丁香) and_ clove stalks _(the peduncles). Status:_ fully classical in Chinese medicine _(丁香), Ayurveda _(लवङ्ग lavaṅga), _and Tibb _(قرنفل qaranful); _attested in the Greco-Roman world only as a late imported aromatic (Pliny's_ caryophyllon_) and becoming major only in medieval European medicine and the spice trade. A cross-linguistic curiosity threads the whole entry: nearly every language names the clove for its_ nail shape.

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Syzygium aromaticum_ (L.) Merr. & L.M.Perry, family Myrtaceae; older and still-encountered synonyms _Eugenia caryophyllata_ Thunb., _E. aromatica_ (L.) Baill., _Caryophyllus aromaticus_ L. The drug is the *dried unopened flower bud* (_Caryophylli flos_ / _Flos Caryophylli_). The species/synonym epithet _caryophyllata_ and the old genus _Caryophyllus_ are from Greek *καρυόφυλλον (karyóphyllon)* — _káryon_ (κάρυον, "nut") + _phýllon_ (φύλλον, "leaf"), "nut-leaf," the ancient Greek-Latin name for the spice; the same word gives English *gillyflower* (a clove-scented pink) through Old French _gilofre_. _Syzygium_ is from Greek _syzygos_ (σύζυγος, "yoked together, paired"), for the paired leaves.#footnote[Botanical synonymy (_Syzygium aromaticum_ ≡ _Eugenia caryophyllata_ ≡ _Caryophyllus aromaticus_): standard authorities (_Plants of the World Online_, Kew). Etymology of _caryophyllon_ / _karyóphyllon_ ("nut-leaf") and its descent to "gillyflower": Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_ (s.v. κάρυον, φύλλον); Liddell & Scott; _Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "gillyflower," "caryophyllaceous"). The old genus _Caryophyllus_ L. is the same word.]

*English:* _Clove_ — from Old French *clou (de girofle)*, "nail (of clove)," from Latin *clāvus*, "nail" — the dried bud looking exactly like a small tack. So English names the spice for its *nail* shape, while _girofle_ / _gillyflower_ preserves the Greek "nut-leaf." Two etymological layers, two ancient names, one spice.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "clove, n.¹"): from Old French _clou de girofle_, "nail of clove," Latin _clāvus_ "nail," from the shape; distinct from "clove" of garlic (a different Old English word, _clufu_). The _girofle_ element < medieval Latin _gariofilum_ < Greek _karyóphyllon_.]

*Chinese:* *丁香 (dīngxiāng)*, "nail aromatic" (丁 dīng = nail/spike; 香 xiāng = fragrance) — *the identical nail metaphor as the Latin/French*. The tradition distinguishes two drug-forms: *公丁香 (gōng dīngxiāng, "male clove")* = the flower bud (the principal drug), and *母丁香 (mǔ dīngxiāng, "mother clove")* = the ripe fruit, milder. The bud's old Han-dynasty name was *雞舌香 (jīshéxiāng, "chicken-tongue aromatic")* — courtiers held it in the mouth to sweeten the breath before addressing the emperor, a documented Han court usage.#footnote[Character glosses and the 公丁香/母丁香 (bud vs. fruit) distinction: Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004); the 雞舌香 "chicken-tongue" Han-court breath-freshening usage is recorded in Han administrative sources and repeated in later _bencao_ literature (Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ 本草綱目, 1578, 丁香 entry).]

*Sanskrit:* *लवङ्ग (lavaṅga)*; also *देवकुसुम (devakusuma, "god-flower")* and *श्रीसंज्ञा (śrīsaṃjñā)*. _Lavaṅga_ is the pan-Indian name and the source of the Hindi *लौंग (laung)*.#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _lavaṅga_, _devakusuma_); the dravya treatment is in Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Karpūrādi-varga_.]

*Arabic / Persian / Urdu (Tibb):* *قرنفل (qaranful)* — the classical Arabic name (cognate with the Greek _karyóphyllon_ family). Persian: *میخک (mīkhak)*, literally "little nail" — *the same nail metaphor once more*. Urdu/Hakeemi: *لونگ (laung)* (Hindi-derived) and *قرنفل (qaranful)*. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.#footnote[_Qaranful_ (Arabic) and _mīkhak_ ("little nail," Persian) for clove: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II (_qaranful_); the "little nail" Persian etymology parallels the Latin _clāvus_ and Chinese 丁 "nail" — a striking cross-linguistic convergence on the bud's shape.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

Species: _S. aromaticum_ (公丁香, the bud). A principal warming interior drug.

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Warm (溫), tending hot.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Pungent (辛 xīn).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Kidney (腎), and Lung (肺).#footnote[Property, flavor, and channel data: Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, 丁香 entry; systematized in Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Actions & indications:*
- *溫中降逆 (wēn zhōng jiàng nì)* — warms the middle and directs rebellious qi downward; *the premier drug for stomach-cold hiccup (呃逆) and vomiting*, including cold-pattern morning sickness. This is clove's signature Chinese action.
- *溫腎助陽 (wēn shèn zhù yáng)* — warms the Kidney and assists yang; for impotence (陽痿) and cold-damp of the lower burner.
- *散寒止痛 (sàn hán zhǐ tòng)* — scatters cold and stops pain (epigastric and abdominal cold pain).

*Cautions:* Contraindicated in heat patterns and yin-vacuity heat (the drug is hot and drying). Classically part of the *十九畏 (Nineteen Fears)*: 丁香 "fears" (畏) *郁金 (yùjīn, _Curcuma_ / turmeric-tuber)* — the two are traditionally not combined.#footnote[Actions, the hiccup/vomiting signature, and the 丁香畏郁金 pairing among the 十九畏 (Nineteen Antagonisms): _Bencao Gangmu_ 丁香 entry; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (appendix on incompatibilities); Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Species: _S. aromaticum_ (लवङ्ग). A classical spice-drug with a notable cross-system twist in its potency.

*Rasa (taste):* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent), Tikta (तिक्त, bitter).

*Guṇa (qualities):* Laghu (लघु, light), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp).

*Vīrya (potency):* *Śīta (शीत, cooling)* — *a genuine cross-system divergence*: Chinese medicine, Tibb, and Greco-Roman theory all class clove as *hot*, but the Bhāvaprakāśa classes lavaṅga as _śīta vīrya_. The tradition reconciles this by treating the pungency/sharpness as locally warming-carminative while the systemic post-effect is cooling (hence its use in _pitta_-associated thirst, burning, and nausea).

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of lavaṅga, including the _śīta vīrya_ classification: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Karpūrādi-varga_ (Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba); P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II. The śīta reading diverges from the Chinese/Tibb/Galenic "hot" placement — an explicit cross-system divergence, not smoothed over.]

*Prabhāva (specific action):* No distinct prabhāva is classically assigned — lavaṅga's actions follow from its rasa-guṇa-vīrya-vipāka (the śīta-vīrya pungent that is simultaneously _dīpana_ and _pitta_-soothing). The _chardighna_ (antiemetic) potency is often singled out as its most reliable clinical action.

*Doṣa effect:* Kapha-Pitta-śāmaka (pacifies Kapha and Pitta — the śīta reading); may increase Vāta in excess (the rūkṣa-tīkṣṇa edge), though the snigdha guṇa tempers this.

*Karma (actions):*
- _Dīpana, pācana, rocana_ — kindles digestion, digests _āma_, restores appetite.
- _Chardighna_ — antiemetic (converges precisely with the Chinese 降逆止嘔 signature).
- _Kāsahara, śvāsahara_ — relieves cough and dyspnea (classic in cough lozenges).
- _Mukhaśuddhikara / mukhadaurgandhyahara_ — breath-freshening (the _tāmbūla_ / betel-quid use).
- _Tṛṣṇānigrahaṇa, dāhaprashamana_ — quenches thirst and burning (the śīta actions).
- _Vājīkara_ (modest), _krimighna_ (anthelmintic), _hṛdya_ (cardiac-benefiting).#footnote[Karma compiled from Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Karpūrādi-varga_, and standard dravyaguṇa (P. V. Sharma, vol. II). The _chardighna_ (antiemetic) and _tṛṣṇānigrahaṇa_ (thirst-quenching) actions are the most-cited; _vājīkara_ is a minor attribution.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S core saṃhitā, N later nighaṇṭu, F folk, M modern/pharmacological)_

- *(N) Conditional:* high-Pitta bleeding tendency and _raktapitta_ (bleeding disorders) in large or oily-extract dose (the tīkṣṇa quality and, in modern terms, the antiplatelet eugenol); few *named* saṃyoga incompatibilities in the classical texts — lavaṅga is more often a corrective/aromatic than a thing with recorded incompatibilities.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Anticoagulant / antiplatelet interaction* — eugenol inhibits platelet aggregation; caution with warfarin/aspirin and around surgery.
  - *Clove-oil hepatotoxicity* — concentrated clove essential oil is hepatotoxic in overdose (serious pediatric case reports); the bud as spice is safe, the oil is not a casual internal medicine.
  - *Mucosal / contact irritation* — undiluted clove oil irritates oral mucosa and skin.#footnote[The antiplatelet effect of eugenol and clove-oil hepatotoxicity (pediatric ingestion case reports) are documented in modern pharmacology/toxicology — flagged as modern (M), not classical; verify against current literature.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Clove (*قرنفل qaranful*) is fully canonical in Tibb — a leading *hot tonic, cardiac exhilarant, digestive, and aphrodisiac*, and a staple of Unani perfumery. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.

*Arabic:* *قرنفل (qaranful)*. Persian: *میخک (mīkhak, "little nail")*. Urdu/Hakeemi: *لونگ (laung)*, *قرنفل (qaranful)*.

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Dry in the 3rd degree* (Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II) — a strong warming-drying placement, converging with Galen and diverging from the Ayurvedic _śīta_ reading.#footnote[Mizāj and actions of _qaranful_: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II (_qaranful_); Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_; the Hot-Dry 3rd-degree placement is the standard Avicennan classification.]

*Afʿāl (actions):*
- *Muqawwī* — tonic, specifically *muqawwī al-qalb* (cardiac), *muqawwī al-dimāgh* (brain/nerve), *muqawwī al-maʿida wa-l-kabid* (stomach and liver), *muqawwī al-baṣar* (eyesight).
- *Mufarriḥ* — cardiac exhilarant / mood-elevator (a member of the Mufarriḥ cardiac register).
- *Muqawwī al-bāh* — aphrodisiac (a famous Unani clove use).
- *Hāḍim* — digestive; *kāsir li-l-riyāḥ* — carminative; *qābiḍ* — astringent (antidiarrheal).
- *Muʿaṭṭir al-fam* — breath-perfumer; *musakkin li-alam al-asnān* — dental analgesic (the toothache use — the classical correlate of eugenol dental anesthesia).

*Istemālāt (indications):* weak/cold digestion, nausea and vomiting, cold liver and stomach, palpitation and low spirits, sexual debility, cold headache, toothache and oral malodor, weak eyesight.

*Mawāneʿ / Iḥtirāzāt (contraindications):* hot/_ṣafrāwī_ (bilious) temperaments and hot-pattern conditions; provokes headache and, in the hot-constituted, may aggravate bleeding tendencies.

*Muṣliḥ (correctives):* rose-water, gum tragacanth (_kathīrāʾ_), sandalwood, and other cooling aromatics to temper the heat.

*Badal (substitute):* cardamom (_hāl_) and, for the aromatic-warming action, a small quantity of nutmeg/mace.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* clove is *not* a Qurʾānic substance and lacks a firmly-authenticated hadith; it belongs to the *Greco-Arabic (Avicennan) and perfumery register* rather than to Prophetic medicine proper — flagged honestly, though it pervades later Muslim folk-perfume and dental practice.

*Classical Tibb formulas:* *Jawārish* (digestive electuaries — clove as warming carminative), *Maʿjūn* aphrodisiac and cardiac electuaries, *Khamīrah* cardiac preparations, and clove's central role in *ʿiṭr* (attar) perfumery and in Mughal-Unani compound aromatics.#footnote[Tibb qaranful: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II; Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_; _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_; formula classes per AYUSH, _National Formulary of Unani Medicine_. The dental-analgesic use is both classical (Tibb _musakkin li-alam al-asnān_) and modern (eugenol).]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *丁香柿蒂湯 (Dīngxiāng Shìdì Tāng, "Clove and Persimmon Calyx Decoction")* — clove + persimmon calyx (柿蒂) + ginseng + fresh ginger; *the* classical formula for hiccup and vomiting from stomach-cold with qi vacuity. The paradigm of clove's 溫中降逆 action.
- *丁香散 (Dīngxiāng Sǎn)* — clove powders for cold-stomach vomiting and epigastric pain.
- *Clove in warming-tonic and aromatic-stomachic combinations* (e.g. with 砂仁, 白豆蔻) for cold-damp of the middle burner; *母丁香* (mother clove, the fruit) used similarly but milder.
- Typical decoction dose ~1–3 g (clove is potent; small doses).#footnote[Formula sources: _Dīngxiāng Shìdì Tāng_ and clove's warming-stomachic use per Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

=== Ayurveda

- *Lavaṅgādi vaṭī / Lavaṅgādi cūrṇa* — clove-based lozenges and powders for cough, throat, and hoarseness (the _kāsahara/śvāsahara_ register).
- *Lavaṅga taila* — clove oil, for toothache (topical) and as a warming-carminative.
- *In compound digestives and aromatics* — clove is a member of many _cūrṇa_ and _avaleha_ formulations; a standard component of masala/spice blends and of *tāmbūla* (betel quid) for breath and digestion.
- *In chardi (vomiting) and tṛṣṇā (thirst) formulations* — clove-water and clove-decoctions for nausea, thirst, and hiccup.#footnote[Formulation sources: Bhāvaprakāśa and standard Ayurvedic formularies (_Sharangadhara Saṃhitā_ for _cūrṇa_/_taila_/_vaṭī_ methods; _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_). The betel-quid (_tāmbūla_) clove use is classical dietetic practice.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman:* cloves were *not* an early Mediterranean staple; *Pliny* (_Naturalis Historia_ XII) mentions *caryophyllon*, an aromatic "grain" imported from India, prized for fragrance — the earliest Western notice, limited and mostly as a perfume/curiosity. The clove tree is native only to the *Maluku (Moluccas)*, reaching the Mediterranean by the long Indian-Ocean spice route.
- *Medieval European medicine and the spice trade:* cloves became a major medieval drug and one of the most valuable spices — driving the *Portuguese and Dutch (VOC) monopoly wars over the Spice Islands*. Used as a warming stomachic and carminative, a dental analgesic, and a component of *pomanders* and clove-studded oranges (_pomme d'ambre_) carried against plague and bad air; the "*Four Thieves' Vinegar*" plague-prophylactic aromatic vinegars often included clove.
- *Modern pharmaceutical survivals:* *eugenol / zinc-oxide–eugenol (ZOE)* cements and dressings are standard in dentistry — the clean modern descendant of the classical toothache use; clove oil persists as a topical dental analgesic and antiseptic, and eugenol as a pharmaceutical/flavoring and antioxidant.#footnote[Pliny's _caryophyllon_: _Naturalis Historia_ XII (Loeb/Rackham). Moluccan origin, the spice-trade history, and pomander/Four-Thieves usage: standard food- and pharmacy-history literature (e.g. Dalby, _Dangerous Tastes_; Freedman, _Out of the East_). Zinc-oxide–eugenol dentistry: standard dental pharmacology.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

Covered above: *qaranful* as a hot cardiac-and-digestive tonic, aphrodisiac, and dental analgesic; canonical in *Jawārish*, *Maʿjūn*, and *Khamīrah* preparations and central to *ʿiṭr* (attar) perfumery.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Clove is a *hot-humid equatorial tropical* tree — native to the Maluku Islands and now grown chiefly in *Zanzibar and Pemba* (long the world's leading producers), *Madagascar*, *Sri Lanka*, *Indonesia*, and *India*. Its exceptionally high essential-oil content (the highest of the common spices) is a wet-tropical adaptation. See `climates.typ`.

=== Hot-humid equatorial (the home climate) — Maluku, Zanzibar, South India, Sri Lanka

Constitutionally the wet tropics carry *Pitta-Kapha* with external *濕熱 (damp-heat)* on the Spleen and Heart; food spoils fast and digestion is taxed by humidity. *Traditional preparation and cares.* A *warming-pungent, powerfully antimicrobial and carminative spice* is exactly the corrective the classical frameworks predict for damp-heat and _āma_ in a humid climate: clove enters the daily masala, the betel quid (breath and digestion), rice and meat dishes (both flavor and *food-preservation* — the eugenol antimicrobial action), and spiced teas. The Ayurvedic _śīta vīrya_ reading is telling here — a spice used in a hot climate is theorized to *cool systemically* even as it kindles digestion locally, which is precisely how a hot-climate people would want to classify a daily-use hot spice.

=== Temperate zones (adoptive) — Europe, the Near East, China

In cool-temperate climates clove is a *winter warming spice* — mulled wine (_Glühwein_), spiced cider, Christmas baking (the northern-European clove-and-cinnamon register), and the warming-stomachic decoctions of Chinese and Unani medicine for cold-stomach conditions. Here the constitutional logic is the ordinary warming-carminative one (external 寒, cold-damp Spleen), not the tropical damp-heat/preservation logic.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

Clove is now globally ubiquitous, and its two climatic logics can collide: the *preservative-and-digestive* rationale of the wet tropics versus the *winter-warming* rationale of temperate zones. Heavy warming-clove use in already hot-Pitta constitutions or in high summer is a mild _ṛtu_/constitution mismatch (the Ayurvedic _śīta_ classification partly hedges this); the greater modern issue is the *isolated clove oil / eugenol* register — a concentrated hepatotoxic extract used casually far outside any traditional food or measured-medicine context. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Clove is a *spice/medicinal*, used in gram or sub-gram amounts; its nutritional contribution is negligible but its phytochemistry is exceptional.

*Key constituents:*
- *Essential oil — 15–20% of the dried bud* (extraordinarily high, the richest of common spices), dominated by *eugenol (70–90% of the oil)*, with *eugenyl acetate* and *β-caryophyllene*. Eugenol is the molecular basis of nearly all the pharmacology and of the classical dental use.
- *Phenolics / tannins* — gallotannins, flavonoids, phenolic acids; clove has one of the *highest antioxidant capacities (ORAC)* of any food.
- Small amounts of triterpenes (oleanolic acid), sterols.#footnote[Composition (15–20% essential oil; eugenol 70–90% of oil; very high antioxidant capacity): USDA FoodData Central for the food matrix; standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_) and current reviews for the essential-oil chemistry — verify against current literature.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Local anesthetic / analgesic (dental)* — eugenol blocks voltage-gated sodium channels and modulates TRPV1; the mechanism of both the traditional and the modern ZOE toothache use.
- *Antimicrobial / antifungal / antiviral* — broad in vitro activity (the food-preservation and antiseptic register); active against many bacteria and _Candida_.
- *Antioxidant and anti-inflammatory* — high phenolic content; eugenol inhibits COX/prostaglandin pathways.
- *Antiplatelet* — eugenol inhibits platelet aggregation (the anticoagulant caution).
- *Carminative / antispasmodic / antiemetic* — the cross-system digestive action.
- *Insecticidal / larvicidal* — eugenol and clove oil (also used against clove-cigarette-relevant and household pests).

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* the limiting compound is *eugenol*, not bud weight.
- *Culinary use* (whole or ground bud, gram amounts): unrestricted at normal intake.
- *Eugenol ADI:* JECFA acceptable daily intake ~*2.5 mg eugenol/kg body weight/day* — the most-cited reference ceiling.
- *Clove essential oil:* concentrated oil is *hepatotoxic in overdose* — pediatric ingestions of a few millilitres have caused serious hepatic injury and CNS depression; *do not take clove oil neat or in significant internal amounts*. Topical dental use employs small, diluted quantities.
- *Cautions / risk groups:* anticoagulant/antiplatelet therapy and pre-surgery (eugenol antiplatelet effect); infants and small children (oil ingestion); contact/mucosal irritation from the oil; *clove ("kretek") cigarettes* carry the respiratory harms of smoking plus eugenol exposure.
- *Reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice* — the spice is safe in food; the concentrated oil is a genuine poison in overdose and warrants care.#footnote[Eugenol ADI (~2.5 mg/kg bw/day): JECFA evaluations. Clove-oil hepatotoxicity in pediatric ingestion, antiplatelet effect, and clove-cigarette harms: clinical-toxicology and pharmacology literature — verify against current sources. These are reference thresholds, not medical advice; concentrated clove oil should be kept away from children and not ingested undiluted.]

